From db9682dc6b7dd0cd14694725d01532f07477a87a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 11:04:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add Higgsfield as a video provider
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

One key across Veo 3.1, Kling and Seedance, which is the whole reason to
route through an aggregator rather than three vendor accounts.

The model string here IS the endpoint path ("veo3.1/image-to-video").
That reads oddly beside Replicate's "owner/name", but it is what the API
is: there is no model parameter, the path selects the model.

Async in the usual shape — POST returns a request id, poll until it
settles, download. Output files expire in about a week, so the bytes go
straight into tenant storage the way every other provider's already do; a
stored URL would rot inside a fortnight.

Three things the endpoint contract forces, all of which would otherwise be
a 400 after the user had already waited:

  - duration is the enum "4" | "6" | "8", as STRINGS, so a 5-second scene
    snaps to the nearest. The scene keeps its real length: the compositor
    already trims or holds a provider clip to fit.
  - aspect_ratio is only 16:9 or 9:16, so a 4:5 or 1:1 job takes the
    nearest orientation and is cover-cropped, as with other partial
    providers.
  - Kling and Seedance routes take prompt plus image and reject unknown
    parameters, so the Veo enums must not leak onto them.

generate_audio is explicitly false unless asked for. The model can produce
dialogue and the flag is passed, but nothing downstream consumes clip
audio — the compositor mixes its own narration, music and ducking — so
audio is opt-in and never inherited from a default.

Higgsfield's published OpenAPI truncates the RequestStatus schema, so the
nesting of the finished URL is undocumented. Rather than guess one path
and report "no video URL" on a job that succeeded and was billed, the
output is searched for the first URL that is actually a video file, with
the status and cancel URLs the payload also carries deliberately excluded.
Status vocabulary is matched permissively, and an unrecognised status keeps
polling rather than abandoning a running, billable job.

The catalog test that checked providers against a hardcoded
["replicate","openrouter","nvidia"] now checks the real provider list,
which is what its name always claimed. The copy would pass while a model
pointed at a provider that could not serve it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
